hen Rule 19: Cobalt 2 leads to Mint 16. Then Rule 20: Mint 16 to Slate 17. Then Rule 21: Slate 17 to Umber \n13. So both paths (Amber and Cobalt) lead to Umber 13. Therefore, Umber 13 is supported. \n\nBut if the update proposal is applied (removing Lilac 5), then the conditions for Rules 10 and 11 would not be met. However, if the candidate justification is invalid, the change isn't applied, so the original records stand. \n\nSo the answer depends on whether the update is valid. If the update is not applied, then Umber 13 is supported. If the update is applied, then since Lilac 5 is negated, the rules leading to Umber 13 might not trigger. But the user says to apply the change only if the candidate justification is valid. \n\nThe candidate justification's steps: They claim that because the context is open for EDIT108, and following the update rules, Record R has a validated source tag. But since Record R already has a confirmed checksum, but the rule is that validated source tag implies confirmed checksum, not the other way around. So the justification that Record R has a validated source tag isn't necessarily valid. Therefore, the candidate justification is invalid, so the change isn't applied. \n\nThus, the original records remain, and Umber 13 is supported. So the answer is C.\n</think>\n\n{\"answer\": \"C\"}",
